<<change_background "market">>
With time still left in the day, you decide to head to the market in the town square to pick up some needed food for home. 
The capital market was famous for its wide variety of stalls and fresh produce. It was also well known for its large crowds, which you now find yourself struggling to navigate through.
Shifting through the waves of people, you finally stop at an open stall parked in front of a large merchant hall in the background. You begin viewing the catalogue of fresh vegetables for what you want for dinner.
It is then, however, that you feel a hand press onto your shoulders. Turning around, you are met with a familiar face you were hoping to avoid today.
<<sp 2>>
{$redFlagName}: “It’s been a while, {$playerName}. How are you doing?”
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “Oh, hello, Lady Sinclair. I am currently getting groceries right now.”
<<MC false>>
{$redFlagName}: “Really now? And you chose the one place in this market right in front of my building?”
Looking back at the large merchant hall designed with intricate architecture, you realize just how rich Judith must be.
{$redFlagName}: “So, I take it you have considered my proposal?”
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “I am considering it, but I currently need to smelt the ore that I extracted before I can start any project.”
<<MC false>>
{$redFlagName}: “Now that sounds interesting, would you mind if I were to watch you work?”
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “The process is confidential, unfortunately. I won’t be able to show you, but I can tell you what I can say once I’m finished.” 
<<MC false>>
{$redFlagName}: “That’s truly a shame. I’ve taken a liking to you, you know?”
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “I have to ask, why me? The morningstar you posted as a commission is so intricate that even a Royal Blacksmith would have trouble with it, much less an apprentice. And now, for some reason, you believe that I am capable, despite not once seeing my work.” 
<<MC false>>  
{$redFlagName}: “You wish for the truth? Fine. I’ll admit, I actually have seen your work before. It was on your first day when you were showing your work to Isham. I witnessed it by accident when I was leaving another forge, disappointed in my lack of options. The door was left open, and I heard a commotion inside. Curious, I peeked inside, and it was then that I was instantly captivated.”
<<cs 2 5>>
{$redFlagName}: "The potential I saw, dear, it was something to admire. You do not know the gift you have.”
Her words, ominously sincere, speak with fervent desire as she gazes upon you with her passionate red eyes. You have been taught not to fall for such honeyed words of a merchant, ensuring your loyalty is only to your craft. Yet still, why do her words feed some unconscious desire of yours for validation?
<<MC true>>
-> “Your kindness, Lady Sinclair, is something I will never forget. I hope I can be worthy of your words.”
    <<set $redFlagAffinity = $redFlagAffinity + 1>> 
    <<cs 2 1>>
    {$redFlagName}: “Now, that’s more like it. I’m glad to see you are starting to recognize your own potential. There’s no more need to keep questioning it, dear, all you have to do is embrace it.”
-> "I hardly think I’m worthy of such compliments, Lady Sinclair, but I’m happy to hear such words.”
    <<cs 2 0>>
    {$redFlagName}: “I told you before, it’s because I trust that you have what it takes to fulfill exactly what I want. There’s no need to keep questioning it, dear.”
-> “You are dodging my question. What is the real reason?”
    <<set $redFlagAffinity = $redFlagAffinity - 1>> 
    <<cs 2 3>>
    {$redFlagName}: “Did you not listen to what I had to say, or are you simply hard of thinking? I admire the potential of your craft and wish to seize your talent for my commissions. I wish to invest in your skills and capitalize on them before the noble society realizes it.”
<<cs 2 0>>
<<MC false>>
{$redFlagName}: “Now, before I go, I wish to leave you with an invitation. I will be hosting a gallery of Pleiotium equipment crafted from some of the most famous Blacksmiths this kingdom has to offer tomorrow. I do hope you can attend.” 
{$redFlagName}: “It would be a great opportunity for you to cultivate your craft as an apprentice. Not only could you witness the art of famous blacksmiths to learn from, but you will also be introduced to the most influential nobles in the kingdom. No apprentice could hope for more.”
<<destroy 2>>
Saying goodbye to the departing Judith, you leave the market in deep thought about the enticing offer she had presented. Her demeanor towards you, while weird, does offer a lot of benefits if you explore this relationship. Unsure yet if you should attend, you head back home for the day to rest and contemplate.
<<change_background "black background">>
On the second day of the weekend, you realize you had forgotten the keys to your forge at work. Deciding it would be better to save yourself from the shame it would bring to be late to work, you decide to go back to the guild to find it.
Yet no matter where you look, you cannot find the keys anywhere they could be. 
Most likely, Isham may have picked up while closing the guild shop. Knowing that your senior works on the weekends in his free time, you decide to stop by his forge to find him.
<<change_background "personal forge">>
Entering Isham’s forge, you call out his name in hope of a response. 
Inside, you notice a meticulously arranged room, with not a single tool out of place. The anvil was worn and used, but carefully cared for to still be perfectly usable. 
Not seeing your senior, you head back to the door to exit. But just as you were leaving, you noticed a peculiar blueprint lying on his desk.
-> Read it
    <<set $readBlueprint to true>>
    Looking at the blueprint, you see the intricate designs of what can only be described as a warhammer. Notes left by Isham scatter the paper, and signs of repeated redrafting tell that this design was something your senior was deeply passionate about.
    Before you can read any further, you hear footsteps in the hallway outside. Backing away from the desk, you wait till the person outside is out of hearing before you exit the room.
    Whatever it is that you saw, you know Isham would not be happy if he caught you peering into his private writings. You wonder if you will ever bring this up to Isham, or if it will be a secret best left forgotten? 
-> Leave it alone
    <<set $readBlueprint to false>>
    Deciding it to be rude to look at the man’s private writings, you choose to leave it alone. 
    Exiting the room before any misunderstandings could happen, you wonder how important the blueprint is to Isham. Is it something worth bringing up to your senior, or will it be a secret best left forgotten?
<<change_background "hallway">>
However, on your way out, you meet eyes with Silas, the one Isham recommended for a commision. Standing still in the hallway in surprise, neither of you speaks until finally Silas opens their mouth.
<<sp 4>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Excuse me, what were you doing in Isham’s forge?”
<<MC true>>
<<cs 0 3>>
{$playerName}: “I was just looking for him. I wanted to ask more about smelting before I start this coming week.”
<<cs 0 0>>
<<MC false>>
{$loneWolfName}: “He is currently busy…did you see anything in there?”
<<MC true>>
-> Tell the Truth
    <<set $loneWolfAffinity = $loneWolfAffinity + 1>>
    {$loneWolfName}: “That blueprint…he cares deeply about it.”
    {$playerName}: “If you don’t mind me asking, what is it?”
    {$loneWolfName}: “I cannot say, he does not like speaking of it. Besides, this is not my conversation to have.”
    <<MC false>>
    It seems Silas will not talk about the matter. Perhaps you could ask Isham about this when you are free? 
-> Lie
    <<set $loneWolfAffinity = $loneWolfAffinity - 1>>
    Silas looks at you with discerning eyes, as if they already know of your deceit.
    <<cs 4 4>>
    {$loneWolfName}: “Isham doesn’t like it when apprentices poke through his stuff. I suggest you respect that privacy as someone receiving tutelage from him.”
    <<cs 0 4>>
    {$playerName}: “I’ll make sure to remember that…”
    <<cs 0 0>>
    <<cs 4 0>>
    Silas only nods, but you notice in their eyes that they do not trust your words.
    <<MC false>>
    Perhaps it would be best to change the conversation before this gets any worse.
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “I’m curious, though, why are you here on the weekend, Silas? Are you also here for Isham?”
<<MC false>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Indeed. I am to go on a hunt soon, and hoped to ask Isham if he could help me identify remnants that were reported to be left behind. Though I am unsure if he will accept something so last-minute.”
{$playerName}: “Would any blacksmith work for the job?”
<<cs 4 4>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Yes, but I…do not know anyone else.”
{$playerName}:“No one?
<<cs 4 0>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Yes.”
{$playerName}: “Well…you know me now.”
<<cs 4 1>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Would you help me in this hunt for a named Iron Monster?”
<<cs 0 3>>
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “Yes, I would be glad to–wait, sorry, a what now?”
<<MC false>>
{$loneWolfName}: "A named Iron Monster called the—”
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “Those walking disasters? The ones told in tales to scare children?”
<<MC false>>
{$loneWolfName}: “Indeed.”
<<cs 0 4>>
<<MC true>>
{$playerName}: “I’ll have to uh…consider the risks first before I join you on that.”
<<MC false>>
{$loneWolfName}: “You need not worry, a blacksmith would not be involved in combat. I only need one to identify the unique metal remnant left behind. That way, I can help hunt the monster successfully.”
<<destroy 4>>
Thanking them for the reassurance, you swiftly exchange goodbyes with Silas as you depart from the guild. Sure, you became a blacksmith to help the kingdom, but hunting a named Iron Monster? You find that to be something way out of your league. That is…unless helping Silas really is that easy? 
<<if $weekOneResult >= 2>>
Due to the result of your efforts, you have earned enough spare time on your weekend to meet with someone for the start of next week.
Who do you wish to spend more time with?
-> Judith 
    <<set $redFlagAffinity = $redFlagAffinity + 1>>
    <<change_background "banquet hall">>
    You decide that you will accept Judith’s invitation, attending her showcase, which she will be hosting tonight.
    The manor, elaborately designed and grandiose in stature, was a sight to behold as you were led to the banquet hall inside. Within, you hear the sounds of eloquent music play from the instruments of skilled musicians as nobles danced in their luxurious outfits.
    The showcase would begin shortly after the ball had concluded.
    Watching the party, you find Judith approaching you with a smile.
    <<sp 2>>
    <<cs 2 1>>
    {$redFlagName}: “Why, it is so good to you have come, {$playerName}.”
    <<cs 2 0>>
    {$redFlagName}: “Come, the showcase will begin shortly. Let me introduce you to some acquaintances of mine.”
    From barons to merchants to even a marquis, Judith spared no effort to parade you to her confidants, bragging about your skill and potential.
    The showcase shortly began afterwards, with the works of famous Royal blacksmiths being presented in a flaunting of wealth to its guests. Lady Sinclair wasted no effort in making sure no equipment went unnoticed. 
    {$redFlagName}: “Do you see, dear, of what Pleiotium can achieve? Nobles of high birth, who are so proud of their lineage, step aside to spectate in the background of those with true power. Wealth and nobility may make you appear powerful, but it is truly skill that has made this kingdom flourish.”
    {$redFlagName}: “This land would have been reduced to nothing if it were not for the Steelheart Knight, who, both smith and knight, single-handedly saved the kingdom. So why is it that instead of meritocracy, we still serve under a feudal government that practices nepotism on the regular?”
    <<MC true>>
    -> “If that is what you believe, Lady Sinclair, why did you introduce me to these nobles?”
        <<set $redFlagAffinity = $redFlagAffinity + 1>>
        <<cs 2 1>>
        {$redFlagName}: “Ever the astute one, dear. I like that about you.”
        {$redFlagName}: “To answer your question, (MC Name), I befriend those with power to ensure I too can gain strength. Whether it be like-minded merchants or Nobles blinded by greed, they can be easily used to ensure that what I desire can be achieved. Only then can real change be brought.”
        Judith speaks of social revolution and a change to the current system, but her words fail to hide the ambition for power behind them. Still, her words carry a passion captivating to listen to. Perhaps it is merely just a quirk of hers to speak so oddly of power?
    -> “I agree with your thinking, Lady Sinclair."
        {$redFlagName}: “It is nice to know you are not a fool who serves gladly to those unfitting.”
        {$redFlagName}: “We must stick together, for the higher nobility will gladly torch these lands and everyone within than give up their power lightly.”
        {$redFlagName}: “Together, however, I think we can have an actual chance at change.”
        Judith smiles, an enchanting one that can captivate any heart. Perhaps you can contribute something meaningful to this kingdom if you work with her. Maybe, just maybe, you too can be remembered as a hero like the Steelheart Knight.
