    <<cs 4 0>>
    You venture to Stalwart Lake, where a group of other hunters reported they last saw the named Iron Monster called the Brass Vermin.
    A rat-like creature standing at around eight-feet tall, it supposedly had claws long and sharp enough to cut down trees. Hunters of lesser renown injured and tracked it down until they came across Stalwart Lake. There, a piece of metal suspected to be a piece of its flesh was found.
    To ensure that the lake is safe for any fishers and visitors, Silas departed to join the hunt for the Brass Vermin, where they will need your help to identify the metal and whether it is a part of the Iron Monster or not.
    {$loneWolfName}: “An Iron Monster is nothing more than a beast simply harder to pierce with regular weapons. Any hunter with a weapon made of Pleiotium could handle them with ease. A named Iron Monster, however, have intellect matching its power. An inexperienced hunter could easily fall prey to its trap.”
    Riding within a carriage, Silas explains to you what they know about the Iron Monsters as you head to your destination.
    <<MC true>>
    {$playerName}: “How come the Royal Knights are not called to handle this Brassed Vermin than?”
    <<MC false>>
    {$loneWolfName}: “If the Knights were called for every monster, the kingdom would never have an army big enough to fight back the army of the Young Iron King if he ever invaded.”
    <<MC true>>
    {$playerName}: “Wasn’t that ancient demon just a myth?”
    <<MC false>>
    {$loneWolfName}: “Who knows. The named Iron Monsters seem to believe in it enough. Same as those cultists that appear every now and then from within. That’s enough for the kingdom to believe it to be possible.”
    {$loneWolfName}: “That is why Hunters are needed, to handle the individual monster that splinters from the hordes. Just like this Brassed Vermin. We don’t know its intentions, but it cannot be allowed to exist so close to civilization.”
    {$loneWolfName}: “It appears we are here. Get ready, I will need your help shortly.”
    <<change_background "lake">>
